  {
   "id": "common-pitfalls",
   "title": "common pitfalls",
   "body": [
    {
     "kind": "list",
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Confusing 1sg -(I)m with 3sg -(s)I."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Evim"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " means «my house»; "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "evi"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " means «his/her house». The 3sg suffix on a consonant-final stem is just "
         },
         {
          "kind": "mono",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "-I"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " (no leading consonant), so it is shorter than the 1sg form. Keep the two distinct."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Dropping the -s- buffer in 3sg after a vowel-final stem."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " The 3sg suffix after a vowel is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "mono",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "-sI"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", not "
         },
         {
          "kind": "mono",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "-I"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ". Writing "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "*arabaı"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is incorrect; the correct form is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "arabası"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ". The buffer consonant -s- prevents two vowels from colliding."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Putting the possessive suffix on the possessor instead of the possessed noun."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " In Turkish, the possessor takes the "
         },
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "genitive suffix"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " and the possessed noun takes the "
         },
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "possessive suffix"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ". "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ahmet'in arabası"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is correct (genitive on Ahmet, possessive on araba), not "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "*Ahmet'im araba"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Inserting a buffer vowel after a vowel-final stem."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " The buffer vowel "
         },
         {
          "kind": "mono",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "(I)"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " only appears after consonant-final stems. After a vowel-final stem, it is dropped. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "*Arabaım"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " and "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "*kapıım"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " are both wrong; the correct forms are "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "arabam"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " and "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "kapım"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Mixing up the two “their” patterns."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " After "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "onların"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "kapısı"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " (their door) is correct and is the usual form for one thing. Without "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "onların"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", though, "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "kapısı"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " means «his/her door» — for «their door» use "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "kapıları"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}